{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310817,"nonce":"n3iI5CFqk6kZPEhWha6wRQXU1oc=","title":"WASM threading lever landed (b50b851) — host-side parallel quant matmul over shared memory; clean tok/s measurement blocked by heap-layout ...","message":"**RESOLVED 2026-06-16 (commit c0e6da5, branch wasm-default-inguest-matmul) — host matmul is a NET LOSS; default flipped to in-guest.** The \"per-host-call overhead\" theory below (730µs/call) is OVERTURNED by a clean two-point decode measurement (prefill cancelled: `decode_ms=(T48−T16)/(tok48−tok16)`, --no-cache, long prompt, M1 Pro). 4-way: in-guest-1T **117ms**, host-1T **239ms** (2.04× slower SINGLE-THREADED, zero pool/FFI-fanout), in-guest-7T **29.9ms**, host-7T **94.2ms** (3.15×). Per-FLOP, not per-call: host-1T is 2× in-guest-1T with ONE thread and ONE call/matmul. ROOT = the native worker threads stream the **quantized weights out of the wasm linear memory** (raw host ptrs into the pinned 4GiB mmap) instead of native heap; the guest's JIT'd SIMD128 loads of the same bytes are 2× faster. So \"native NEON kernel via FFI\" LOSES to the guest kernel despite better codegen. FIX = host offload (matmul + flash) now **opt-in** via `RAYZOR_WASM_HOST_MATMUL=1`; default = in-guest. Default wasm decode **86.2→25.6 ms/step (3.37×)**, France→Paris unchanged. Adversarial workflow (wf_714beefd) predicted per-flop + proposed a native-heap weight-MIRROR to make host competitive (host-1T→~native-22ms → 7T ~6-9ms = 110-165 tok/s IF the slowness is purely mem-read not FFI) — the UNTESTED deeper lever, bigger change (ABI + ~0.7GB mirror). Until then in-guest is the win. ALSO: the \"OOB in wasm qmatmul at prefill\" / \"heap-layout load trap\" below was **STALE BLADE CACHE** — long prompts trap on a reused `.rayzor`, work 100% with `--no-cache`. The \"degenerate France→best-answer\" correctness note below is ALSO stale: current in-guest + host both emit \"Paris.\" Always debug wasm llama with `--no-cache`.\n\nWASM quant matmul threading landed at **commit b50b851** (2026-06-13). This is the \"threads = 8x lever\" from the wasm perf arc, implemented as **host-side threads** (Architecture B), not guest wasi-threads (rejected: single wasmtime Store is !Sync, no wasi_thread_spawn in this embedding).\n\n**Architecture.** The guest threaded matmul (`rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded` + qkv variant in `runtime-wasm/src/qtensor.rs`) allocates `y`, then calls a NEW host import `rayzor_host_qmatmul_par` passing raw wasm byte-offsets + dims (NO struct remap — guest extracts scalars). The host (`host_parallel_qmatmul` in `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_runner.rs`) prequantises x→Q8_K natively (`prepare_x_q8k_blocks_into`) and fans the `n` output columns across the native worker pool (`rayzor_runtime::worker_pool::global().parallel_rows`, the SAME pool the native 30-tok/s decode uses). Q4_K_M dots use the native NEON SDOT kernel `dot_q4_k_q8_kblock_llamacpp` (same Q8KBlock activation, cfg-gated on aarch64+dotprod, scalar `vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K` fallback). Q6_K still uses the scalar dispatcher `vec_dot_q6_K_q8_K` — its SDOT path (`dot_q6_k_q8`) needs the Q8Block activation (different prequant), left as a follow-up.\n\n**Wiring details (so you don't rediscover them):**\n- Linker emits rt.func_imports verbatim with their module/name; ALL index math derives from `rt.func_imports.len()`, so adding one runtime-wasm import shifts consistently (n_wasi_imports 4→5). Guest declares it `#[cfg(target_arch=\"wasm32\")] #[link(wasm_import_module=\"rayzor\")] extern \"C\"`.\n- Host registers it via `linker.func_new(\"rayzor\", \"rayzor_host_qmatmul_par\", ...)` AFTER `let mut registered` and `registered.insert(...)` so the final stub loop (the \"Failed to stub … defined twice\" path) skips it. The tensor-fallback loop is short-circuited when runtime-wasm provides tensors, so register OUTSIDE it.\n- Memory base: `caller.data().shared_memory.clone() → shared.data().as_ptr()`. Stable because `memory_may_move(false)`. Soundness: guest blocked in the call, bands write disjoint `y`, read-share weights+x. Raw ptrs cross the `parallel_rows` `'static+Send+Sync` boundary as `usize`, reconstructed inside the closure.\n- compiler now has a DIRECT `rayzor-runtime-core` dep (was transitive).\n- `RAYZOR_WASM_NO_WGPU=1` skips the unused wgpu/Metal init on the CPU path.\n- Marked `vec_dot_q6_K_q8_K` `unsafe` (derefs raw ptr; clippy `not_unsafe_ptr_arg_deref` is deny-level here).\n\n**CORRECTNESS — CRITICAL CORRECTION (2026-06-13):** The wasm llama output is DEGENERATE and always has been. \"What is the capital of France?\" → \"The best answer is :\" is GARBAGE (right answer: Paris); long prompts → \"The best answer is:\" repeated forever. I (and the prior wasm-arc commits 3a017fd→b50b851) mistook this stable-but-degenerate output for \"canonical\" and optimized a numerically-broken path. NEVER compared wasm to native. **The bug is NOT the matmul** (b50b851 is sound): weights are correct raw Q4_K_M/Q6_K GGUF blocks; `prepare_x_q8k_blocks_into` delegates to `quantize_row_q8_K` (matmul.rs:206) — the SAME prequant native uses — and the host Q4 dot `dot_q4_k_q8_kblock_llamacpp` is the SAME kernel native uses coherently. So the host Q4 path == native by construction. The degenerate output is a **PRE-EXISTING wasm forward-pass bug** (KV cache / RoPE position / tensor slice / attention / prompt-feed in runtime-wasm or wasm codegen). KEY SIGNAL: output is IDENTICAL across totally different prompts (Voronoi vs France) → the model isn't conditioning on input at all → structural forward bug, not noisy numerics. CAVEAT: could not run a clean host-on/off A/B — the heap-layout load trap (see below) blocks loading on a busy machine (load 11-20); on a quiet machine it loaded 5/5 per d8d9180. NEXT: compare each wasm runtime tensor op (embedding gather, flash_attn, rmsnorm, rope, kv append/slice, broadcast) vs its native counterpart; verify on a quiet machine. Host path IS engaged (faster than guest sequential) but that's moot until forward correctness is fixed.\n\n**MEASUREMENT BLOCKED — no clean threading number yet.** Two compounding blockers on 2026-06-13: (1) machine was at load 13–25 the whole session (user's rust-analyzer/cargo on other projects) → worker threads timeshare, win masked; conflated 5-token metric only reached 2.3–2.9 tok/s. (2) A pre-existing **heap-layout-dependent load-time `unreachable` trap** at `nue.loader.GGUFLoader.metadataFromReader` (before ANY matmul) fires nondeterministically and is **prompt-length-sensitive** — the short \"France\" prompt loads (but EOSes at 5 tokens, no decode signal), longer prompts that would generate 100+ tokens trap 5/5. Same forward-ref-stub/heap-layout class as [[bugs_w0020_value_map_miss_cross_loop]] / test_copy_clone_identity. To get a real decode tok/s: run on an IDLE machine with a short-ish prompt that still generates many tokens, OR fix the load trap first. Expected win if threading scales like native: matmul is ~98.7% of wasm forward (measured), 7 compute threads → Amdahl ~6x over the 1.18 single-thread baseline. The 2.9 floor was under load 20.\n\n**Prefill batching + per-call overhead (2026-06-14, commit 762b15f).** Measured native vs wasm with RAYZOR_PROFILE_DECODE on a 35-tok prompt: NATIVE prefill 7.7ms/tok (faster than its 10.5ms decode — batched GEMM amortises), WASM prefill 82ms/tok (== its decode rate, NOT batched). Batched the host matmul over prompt rows (one prequant + one parallel_rows over n with inner batch loop, mirroring native qmatmul_chunk_impl_sdot_q4km_batch) — but only ~5% win (2.88→2.76s prefill). ROOT CAUSE of the 8-10× per-token gap is NOT batching: it's PER-HOST-CALL overhead. host-on vs host-off: host matmul gives 2.6× (prefill 7.0→2.71s, decode 196→82ms) so it IS engaged + helping, but each host-matmul call costs ~730µs (82ms / ~112 matmul calls/token) vs native ~94µs/call — same NEON SDOT kernel + same worker_pool, so the gap is the call path (guest→host boundary + shared_memory.data() + parallel_rows fan-out from a re-entrant host import, ~12× native's fan-out cost). REAL LEVERS for wasm decode/prefill speed: (1) cut per-host-call overhead (why is parallel_rows ~12× costlier from a host import? worker wake / caller-assist / shared_memory.data() per call), (2) reduce host-call count by fusing more matmuls, (3) host-side the attention — wasm decode now uses the in-wasm bmm+softmax+bmm chain (the [[bugs_wasm_stringmap_dup_stub]] attention fixes), NOT the fused flash_attn_decode native uses. Follow-ups: (a) Q6_K host SDOT (`dot_q6_k_q8` + Q8Block prequant) — ffn_down + lm_head are Q6_K (~30% of weights), currently host-scalar. (b) the heap-layout load trap (separate deep bug). Related: [[bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak]] (30GB OOM still open for long gens).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_wasm_host_parallel_matmul` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}